The '''History of Guajicutea (2000–2012)''' covers the end of [[Julián Mera]]'s long rule, the May Revolution that followed his death, and the restoration-era politics that emerged after the collapse of the PUP single-party system. The period began with mass protests, an emergency election under [[Adonian Community]] observers, and a new government that promised accountability for disappearances and secret prisons. It ended with the rise of the [[Partido Bukaí|Partido Bukaí]] in 2012 after the [[Partido Restauración]] gradually lost support.

== The death of Mera and the May Revolution ==

[[Julián Mera]] died on April 14, 2000. His state funeral drew a huge public crowd and briefly gave the island a sense of unity, but the funeral also exposed the depth of the PUP succession crisis. During the last years of Mera's rule, most other parties had disappeared from serious national politics and the PUP had operated as the only effective party since the 1992 election. Once Mera was gone, factional fighting broke out immediately.

Mera's son, [[Emilio Mera]], took control of the PUP apparatus, but a rival figure, [[Raúl Makuán]], broke away and formed his own party, the [[Partido Restauración]]. Makuán presented himself as a corrective to the corruption and exhaustion of the Mera system, but he was arrested on May 11 and found dead in his cell the next day. Foul play was widely suspected.

The death set off a month of mass protests, street confrontations, and political strikes that became known as the [[May Revolution (Guajicutea)|May Revolution]]. The unrest shattered the old party discipline. The PUP split into the remnant [[Nuevo PUP]] and the new [[Partido Restauración]], while the old aura of stability around the Mera government collapsed.

== The caretaker government and the July election ==

With the country in crisis, the vice president, [[Lucía Herrera]], took over as caretaker leader. Herrera was a moderate inside the PUP, and she moved quickly to restore public order without widening the violence. She called for new elections to be held on July 8, 2000 and requested [[Adonian Community]] observation for the vote. The AC mission sent election observers, which gave the process more legitimacy than the emergency months that preceded it.

The July election reopened national politics. [[Kadarist Party of Guajicutea|KdG]] returned as a serious force, along with a [[Partido Yayaí|Partido Yayaí]] and a new labor party that had both been absent from the narrow PUP-dominated politics of the 1990s. The election gave the [[Partido Restauración]] a majority, and [[Andrés Paredes]] won the presidency as its new leader.

== The restoration government ==

Paredes came to power promising a public reckoning. His government opened investigations into Makuán's death, the disappearances of journalists, and the missing members of opposition and party organisations during the previous decade. The inquiries uncovered a secret police unit created during the 1990s to monitor dissidents, move prisoners through off-site detention facilities, and eliminate those who were not supposed to be seen again.

The investigation led to the discovery of mass graves near those facilities. More than 5,000 dissidents were eventually identified or counted in the exposed burial sites. The revelations transformed the memory of the Mera era. What had looked to supporters like a period of stability became, in public memory, a period of concealment and violence.

In response, the government established an annual memorial day on April 14, the date of Mera's death, to honour those who died under the Mera regime and to mark the victims of the disappeared. The day became one of the central dates in Guajicutean civic memory.

== Politics in the 2000s ==

The restoration government governed a more open system than the late PUP years, but politics remained tense. The free press was still recognised, and newspapers remained legally active, yet the state kept many of the surveillance and control habits built in the 1990s. Journalists continued to be arrested, union protest was still tightly constrained, and the judiciary had been reshaped by Mera's earlier purges. Search-and-seizure laws also continued to work around older privacy protections.

The government still faced the legacy of the old executive machine. During the Mera years, the courts had been packed with loyalists, and the press had been pushed toward neoliberal editorial lines. That structure did not vanish overnight. The new order allowed criticism, but it did so inside a narrower space than before the crisis.

Paredes gradually lost support over the decade. By 2008, the [[Partido Restauración]] no longer had enough strength to govern alone and had to rule in coalition with the [[Partido Yayaí|Partido Yayaí]]. The coalition was practical rather than enthusiastic: it reflected the fact that the old one-party system was gone, but no new bloc had yet become dominant enough to replace it cleanly.

In 2012, the [[Partido Bukaí|Partido Bukaí]] won the presidency and the party election, ending the long restoration-era hold of the [[Partido Restauración]]. The result marked a new political turn rather than a return to the old PUP order. By then, the island had moved through two different post-Mera systems: first the emergency restoration, then a broader coalition politics that finally gave way to a new liberal cycle.

== Public mood ==

The public mood in the early 2000s mixed relief, anger, and exhaustion. Many people welcomed the exposure of the disappearance system and the return of open elections, but the revelations also deepened distrust in the state. Older Guaji remembered the Mera years as stable but constricting; younger Guaji saw them as a period of arrested movement, fear, and delayed justice.

Tourism had begun recovering in the 1990s and continued to improve through the 2000s, so the economy was steadier than it had been in the crisis years. Crime had started to fall by 1992 and kept easing through the decade, though it remained a major political issue. The country was less violent than in the late 1970s, but the memory of repression made people cautious about power.

== Economy and employment ==

Mera's late years had hidden a growing fiscal problem. The government kept routine spending afloat by selling assets, especially airports and ports, so the budget looked healthier than it was. By 2002, the state was in severe debt. The restoration government negotiated a new [[Adonian Community]] loan, restructured obligations, and issued new bonds to roll the debt forward.

Tourism remained the anchor sector, but the state no longer tried to rely on tourism alone. The recovery strategy moved toward a broader services economy: call centers, reservations, back-office accounting, customer support, and other offshoring work for Sartan firms. Lower labour costs made Guajicutea attractive to Sartan companies, and bilingual urban workers became an important asset. Tourism still mattered, but it now sat beside a growing services sector rather than standing alone.

Employment shifted accordingly. Hospitality and transport recovered first, then call centers, administration, logistics, retail, and construction around airport and resort corridors. The change did not remove inequality, but it did create a more stable base of wage work than the crisis-era economy had offered.

