== Culture ==

The culture of the 2000s was shaped by tourism, recovery politics, and the memory of repression. Urban culture became more consumer-oriented, especially in coastal districts, where hotels, restaurants, nightlife, and resort districts set the pace. At the same time, newspapers and television remained the main public cultural systems, and major outlets increasingly argued for neoliberal development, private investment, and tourism-led growth.

The restoration period also produced a more anxious public culture. Journalists, artists, and writers kept returning to the disappearances, the secret prisons, and the mass graves uncovered after 2000. Memorial practice around April 14 became part of civic life. School curricula, documentaries, and public ceremonies emphasized the victims of the Mera era, while the state tried to balance remembrance with economic recovery. Younger urban Guaji grew up in a country that was less openly repressive than the 1990s, but still cautious, commercially oriented, and heavily shaped by resort culture.

== Demographics ==

The population continued to grow, but much more slowly than in the baby-boom era. The country became even more urban, with growth concentrated in the capital, the northern coast, resort districts, and the airport-linked corridor.

