The age profile of the country changed the tone of politics. The baby-boom generation remained powerful, but its members were no longer youthful protest voters; they had become middle-aged officeholders, union leaders, business owners, and civil servants. Younger adults formed a smaller share of the population than they had in the 1970s, which helped lower the pressure that had driven the crisis years.

